WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.612
COUNT looks simple but there are several forms and
they answer different

00:00:03.638 --> 00:00:09.138
questions COUNT star counts input rows It does not
care whether a

00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:13.475
particular column is NULL If a row exists in the
input set

00:00:13.638 --> 00:00:17.525
COUNT star includes it COUNT of a column counts only
the rows

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:22.288
where that particular column is not NULL In the
admissions example COUNT

00:00:22.312 --> 00:00:26.125
discharge_time can be interpreted as the number of
admissions that have a

00:00:26.138 --> 00:00:30.850
recorded discharge time Rows with a missing
discharge time are ignored by

00:00:30.875 --> 00:00:36.388
that expression COUNT DISTINCT column goes one step
further It removes

00:00:36.412 --> 00:00:41.925
duplicate non-NULL values before counting COUNT
DISTINCT subject_id therefore

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:45.375
tells us how many different patients appear in the
admissions table not

00:00:45.388 --> 00:00:49.888
how many admission rows exist Run the example and
compare the three

00:00:49.912 --> 00:00:55.575
numbers Ask what population each number represents
All admissions

00:00:55.738 --> 00:00:59.338
completed admissions and unique patients are related
but they are not the

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:04.950
same metric This distinction is important in real
analysis A report

00:01:04.975 --> 00:01:08.188
can be wrong even when the SQL runs successfully if
the analyst

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.162
chooses the wrong form of COUNT Whenever you see
COUNT try to

00:01:12.175 --> 00:01:14.850
finish the sentence I am counting what exactly
